course: dictionary
lesson: 5161
title: Irrigation: water for crops
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Irrigation is a noun, and the words around it give you clues about its meaning. Read «bringing water to fields is called irrigation». The clue is bringing water to fields, so irrigation means getting water to the land.
explain: Look at the next clue: «in deserts, with too little rain, crops cannot grow without it.» Too little rain and crops cannot grow tell you that irrigation is the way water reaches plants when rain is not enough. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Try watering in place of irrigation and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Farmers dug canals to carry river water to their fields, which is called irrigation.» The canals and river water show that irrigation means moving water from a river to the fields. Irrigation can also mean washing out a wound in medicine, but the clues here are about fields and crops, so the farming meaning fits.
fact: Irrigation means supplying water to land and crops through pipes, channels or sprinklers so plants can grow.
fact: In medicine, irrigation means washing out a wound or part of the body with liquid.
fact: «Canals» are channels dug to carry water, and ancient peoples dug them for irrigation.
fact: Farming uses about 70 percent of all the fresh water people take from rivers, lakes and underground.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «irrigation system» and «drip irrigation».
exercise: «Farmers dug canals to carry river water to their fields, which is called irrigation.» What is irrigation here? | *Carrying water to fields so crops can grow | Catching rain in a bucket for drinking | Digging holes to bury seeds | Cutting down trees for wood
exercise: In a desert, with too little rain, what can irrigation help? | *Crops to grow | Camels to swim | Rivers to freeze | Sand to blow away
exercise: A nurse uses clean liquid to wash out a deep cut. What is this medical use called? | Drought | *Irrigation | Harvest | Canal
test: «Farming uses about 70 percent of all the fresh water people take from rivers, lakes and underground.» Which source is NOT named in this sentence? | Underground | Rivers | Lakes | *Oceans
test: Ancient peoples dug canals for irrigation. Why did they dig them? | To hide from enemies | To make roads for carts | *To bring water to crops | To store fish for winter
exam: Which sentence best shows the farming meaning of irrigation? | A child washes her hands with soap | *Farmers pipe river water into dry fields so crops can grow | The sun sets early in winter | A bird flies over the hill at dawn
